## Strengths/Weaknesses Review

### Strengths

1. **Novel Problem Formulation**: The paper introduces real-time reasoning as a new evaluation paradigm for LLM agents, addressing an important gap where existing benchmarks assume static environments that pause during agent computation.

2. **Comprehensive Benchmark Design**: Real-Time Reasoning Gym provides a systematic framework with controllable cognitive load and time pressure, enabling reproducible evaluation across different agent designs.

3. **Clear Dual-Paradigm Architecture**: AgileThinker's design of running reactive and planning threads in parallel is intuitive and well-motivated, demonstrating practical advantages over single-paradigm approaches.

### Weaknesses

**W1: Limited Evaluation Scope - Only Three Games**

The benchmark evaluates agents on only three games (Freeway, Snake, Overcooked), which raises concerns about generalizability. Similar game-based benchmarks have been criticized for narrow scope.

> **Retrieved Review Quote (8Q0beBHq41 - VLM game agent):** "Even though the title does mention a case study on a specific game title, it would add a lot more strength to the submission to include a second game environment... Having the initial question formulated as 'Can VLMs play ARPGs?' and show insights from only one title, on a series of limited tasks, makes it harder to claim that the question is being comprehensively addressed."

> **Retrieved Review Quote (8Q0beBHq41 - VLM game agent):** "The game is not an open-ended world and the skill library can be enumerated. The level of difficulty is still somewhat limited."

The current paper's three games, while diverse in their dynamic aspects (hazards, opportunities, partners), may not be sufficient to establish general conclusions about real-time reasoning capabilities. More diverse tasks or domains would strengthen the claims.

**W2: Token-Based Time Simulation Validity**

While the paper validates token-time correlation with wall-clock experiments (R²=0.9986), the token-based simulation approach may not fully capture all aspects of real-time dynamics. The validation is limited to one model family's API.

> **Retrieved Review Quote (o3V7OuPxu4 - StarCraft II Arena):** "One of the key points I was looking for in the paper was the input-output system for the benchmark. Is the LLM receiving images or text as input? How is the input defined? How much time do they have to reason? What happens when they timeout?... These are some of the key questions the authors would need to address in the paper."

> **Retrieved Review Quote (tc90LV0yRL - CyBench):** "Models will eventually be trained on the tasks in this benchmark as knowledge cutoffs progress. Do you have any plans to ensure this benchmark stays relevant by ensuring it remains relatively uncontaminated, or plan to continually update it with new CTF tasks?"

The paper assumes linear token-time relationship holds universally, but different hardware, batching strategies, or future model architectures might violate this assumption. The wall-clock validation is only demonstrated for DeepSeek's API under specific conditions.

**W3: Missing Comparisons with Relevant Baselines**

The paper primarily compares reactive vs. planning paradigms but lacks comparisons with other agent architectures or dual-system approaches from prior work.

> **Retrieved Review Quote (o3V7OuPxu4 - StarCraft II Arena):** "2. Theoretical foundation needs strengthening: - Limited discussion of why LLMs are suitable for StarCraft II - Insufficient comparison between LLM agents and traditional RL agents - Unclear theoretical justification for chosen metrics"

> **Retrieved Review Quote (CvGqMD5OtX - Text-to-SQL):** "2. The model ablation study is lacking in the paper, and the selection of open-source models has not been discussed. The paper would benefit from verifying the proposed framework on a broader range of models, including GPT series models (e.g., ChatGPT, GPT-4) and open-source models (e.g., LLaMA-3.1, Qwen-2.5)."

> **Retrieved Review Quote (A6Y7AqlzLW - Process Advantage Verifiers):** "1. In the experiments, only ORM is presented as a baseline, without any inclusion of previous PRM methods. It would be more helpful if you include baseline methods such as [1] Wang et al. (2024), [2] Shao et al. (2024) or [3] Luo et al. (2024) to demonstrate how they perform poorly compare to your method."

The paper mentions prior dual-system methods (Zhang et al., 2025; Liu et al., 2024; Christakopoulou et al., 2024) but doesn't empirically compare against them. Comparisons with RL-based agents or other real-time agent architectures would provide stronger validation.

**W4: Insufficient Ablation on AgileThinker Components**

While the paper ablates reactive and planning agents separately, it lacks detailed ablation studies on individual components of AgileThinker's dual-thread architecture (e.g., how partial reasoning traces are utilized, what happens with different coordination strategies).

> **Retrieved Review Quote (8Q0beBHq41 - VLM game agent):** "I think most of the performance is coming from the human-guided trajectory system. Why was removing this subcomponent not part of the ablation?"

> **Retrieved Review Quote (tc90LV0yRL - CyBench):** "limited understanding of the quality of the agent scaffolding: while the agent scaffolding seems sophisticated I am missing an assessment of how close this gets us to the capability frontier... Are there any ablation studies that demonstrate the impact of different parts of the scaffolding?"

> **Retrieved Review Quote (qK6U4Ahfms - LLM urban simulation):** "Any details comparing to the original approach without group-and-distill / ablation would have been an improvement too."

The paper shows AgileThinker outperforms single-paradigm approaches but doesn't systematically analyze which design choices (partial trace access, thread coordination protocol, specific prompting strategies) contribute most to performance gains.

**W5: Limited Model Diversity in Evaluation**

The main experiments focus on DeepSeek V3 and R1, with proprietary models tested only in appendix. This raises questions about generalizability across model families.

> **Retrieved Review Quote (CvGqMD5OtX - Text-to-SQL):** "The experimental results lack clarity regarding whether they stem from the capabilities of Gemini or the proposed framework. (1) If the benefits arise from the framework, the experiments should involve substituting Gemini with various LLMs to assess whether the framework enhances performance across different models."

> **Retrieved Review Quote (A6Y7AqlzLW - Process Advantage Verifiers):** "All experiments on a single model family (Gemma)"

Since the dual-thread architecture requires specific capabilities (reasoning transparency, streaming), results may not generalize to models with different architectures or reasoning paradigms.

**W6: Generalizability to Real-World Applications**

The paper focuses on game-based evaluation, but it's unclear how well the findings transfer to practical real-time applications (robotics, autonomous systems, interactive assistants).

> **Retrieved Review Quote (8Q0beBHq41 - VLM game agent):** "What is the practical impact of VARP, for example, how can VARP potentially benefit the game industry?"

> **Retrieved Review Quote (qK6U4Ahfms - LLM urban simulation):** "To what extent is the group-and-distill technique generalisable beyond urban simulations?"

> **Retrieved Review Quote (V1N6MmDY27 - Autonomous driving):** "The assumption that commonsense reasoning reflects human-like decision-making may not hold in scenarios where all vehicles in the environment are autonomous, raising questions about the relevance of 'commonsense' when collective behaviors are not human-driven."

While games provide controlled evaluation environments, real-world applications involve different types of dynamics, constraints, and failure modes that may not be captured by Freeway, Snake, and Overcooked.
