## WEAKNESSES

### 1. Unfair Comparison Between Fine-tuned and Zero-shot Models
The paper compares post-trained world models against base policies without world models, which lacks proper baselines. A human reviewer of AdaWM (NEu8wgPctU.md, Reviewer 4) noted a similar concern: **"It is not fair to compare the performance of a model finetuned for a specific task against the pretrained baseline models that have not undergone fine-tuning for that same task... comparison against existing un-finetuned baselines cannot adequately demonstrate the advantage of the proposed method."** World-In-World shows dramatic improvements from post-training (Figure 6: 60.25% to 63.34%), but this gains strength assessment through fair baselines with similarly fine-tuned comparison methods rather than zero-shot models.

### 2. Limited Evaluation Scope and Task Diversity
While claiming to be a "comprehensive" benchmark, World-In-World evaluates on only 4 embodied tasks. The PhysBench benchmark reviewers (Q6a9W6kzv5.md, Reviewer 3) raised this concern: **"The comparison between zero-shot models and fine-tuned models trained on PhysBench raises concerns about fairness... To ensure fair comparisons, the authors should either incorporate more challenging tasks or datasets to match the capabilities of fine-tuned models."** Additionally, the manipulation task results (Table 3) show only modest improvements over baselines (46.5% vs 44.5%), suggesting the benchmark may not adequately test world model utility across diverse interactions.

### 3. Limited Generalization Beyond Simulation
The paper acknowledges but does not adequately address the sim-to-real gap. The PokeFlex dataset reviewers (XwibrZ9MHG.md, Reviewer 4) critiqued similar simulation-only work: **"The authors mention in the paper that existing simulation methods require real-world fine-tuning... However, simulations actually perform very well in tasks such as dropping and poking... The authors need to include validation within simulators to verify whether the meshes scanned in the paper can be used for simulation and to conduct a thorough analysis of the differences between current simulation algorithms and real-world conditions."** World-In-World's purely simulation-based evaluation leaves open questions about whether gains transfer to real robotic systems with noisy sensors and dynamics.

### 4. Incomplete Coverage of Related Benchmarks
The paper mentions WorldModelBench and WorldScore but does not comprehensively compare against existing world model evaluation frameworks. PhysBench reviewers (Q6a9W6kzv5.md, Reviewer 3) identified this issue: **"The paper lacks a comprehensive discussion of related works... it is crucial to compare this work with other 3D spatial VQA benchmarks such as SQA3D, OpenEQA, EmbodiedScan, etc. These benchmarks offer direct evaluations... providing valuable context and highlighting gaps or overlaps."** World-In-World would benefit from more thorough positioning against existing embodied AI evaluation frameworks.

### 5. Computational Overhead Not Adequately Addressed
While Figure 7 shows inference-time scaling benefits, the practical cost remains problematic. An AdaWM reviewer (NEu8wgPctU.md, Reviewer 2) noted: **"The method requires model and policy rollouts, which may introduce significant safety issues in the real world... the simulation domain gap may lead to out-of-distribution problems in real-world applications."** World-In-World allocates 3-11 inferences per episode (Figure 7) but does not analyze whether this computational cost is practical for real-time robotic applications or compare efficiency against simpler baseline policies.

### 6. Post-training Gains May Reflect Domain Adaptation Rather Than Better World Models
The dramatic post-training improvements (Figure 6) raise questions about whether gains reflect better action-conditioned generation or dataset overfitting. PhysBench reviewers (Q6a9W6kzv5.md, Reviewer 1) expressed similar concern: **"The test set of PhysBench is derived from the similar set of images as train. This ensures that any fine-tuning would result in performance improvement. Did the authors test PhysAgent against out of distribution images that are labeled for physical understanding?"** World-In-World post-trains on HM3D training split and tests on validation scenes from the same simulator distribution, which may not isolate whether improvements come from genuine world modeling improvements or domain-specific overfitting.

---

**Note on Strengths:** The paper's unified closed-loop framework, comprehensive evaluation on multiple tasks, and systematic analysis of scaling laws (Figures 6-7) represent solid contributions. The finding that controllability matters more than visual quality (Figure 5) is valuable. However, the weaknesses above suggest the benchmark design and evaluation methodology could be strengthened.
