- The paper makes a well-motivated theoretical contribution by adapting µP (Maximal Update Parametrization) to learned optimizers, with formal propositions proving the desiderata are satisfied.
- The empirical evaluation spans a diverse suite of 35 tasks (MLPs, Vision Transformers, decoder-only language models) with models up to width 8192 and training horizons up to 25,000 steps.
- The key observation that µ-parameterization enables generalization to wider, deeper, and longer optimization targets is novel and practically valuable.

## Weaknesses

1. **Unfair comparison between per-task tuned hand-designed optimizers and architecture-limited learned optimizers.** The paper explicitly states that learned optimizers are meta-trained **only on MLP tasks** while AdamW and µAdam receive **per-task tuning with 500+ configurations per task**. This creates an asymmetry: the hand-designed baselines are specialized to each test task while the learned optimizers are trained exclusively on a narrow task family (MLPs). The claim that µLO "outperforms per-task-tuned AdamW" conflates this architectural generalization gap with the benefit of the parameterization itself. As one reviewer for a related meta-learning paper noted: the setup is highly relevant to domain generalization, and comparing only with appendix-level experiments limits the credibility of the cross-architecture claims. [(S8nFZ98pmU review)](https://github.com/openreview/web/blob/master/iclr2025_data/human_reviews/S8nFZ98pmU.md)

2. **The "zero-cost" framing is misleading.** While the paper claims µ-parameterization adds "no extra computational cost" compared to standard-parametrization meta-training, the actual process of meta-training a µLO still requires significant compute resources (100 GPU hours) and is not a task-agnostic, plug-in replacement for hand-designed optimizers. As a related optimizer paper reviewer observed, "the takeaways of the experiments are vague and imprecise" when performance improvements are claimed without clarifying whether the gains justify the real-world deployment cost relative to simpler alternatives. [(zfeso8ceqr review)](https://github.com/openreview/web/blob/master/iclr2025_data/human_reviews/zfeso8ceqr.md)

3. **The ablation does not fully isolate µ-parameterization from multi-width training.** The paper's key ablation compares µLO_M (µ-parameterization + multi-width training) against LO_M (standard parameterization + multi-width training), but this confounds two changes simultaneously. Whether µ-parameterization alone (without multi-width training) provides meaningful gains over standard parameterization on the same training distribution is not clearly shown. This makes it difficult to attribute the generalization improvements specifically to the parameterization rather than to the training distribution design.
</final_review>
