Based on my review of the paper and retrieval of relevant human reviews, I'll now compose the final weakness assessment.

<final_review>
## Weaknesses

### 1. Simulation-to-Real Domain Gap and Generalization Concerns

The paper evaluates world models entirely within simulated environments (Habitat-Sim, CoppeliaSim, RLBench), raising concerns about whether findings transfer to real-world embodied settings. This aligns with the AdaWM review which notes: *"the simulation domain gap may lead to out-of-distribution problems in real-world applications."* While the paper addresses cross-domain transfer between synthetic and real scenes (Table 6), performance degrades substantially under distribution shift—SVD trained on HSSD achieves only 58.98% AR SR versus 60.98% with HM3D training—suggesting generalization remains a significant limitation.

### 2. Limited Metrics for Physical Dynamics Evaluation

The paper acknowledges that video-based world models struggle with "capturing contact-rich interactions, compliance, friction, and state changes of articulated or deformable objects." This echoes findings from related simulation work that metrics like MSE, PSNR, LPIPS, and FVD fail to measure physical fidelity. The manipulation task results (Table 3) show only marginal gains over baselines for most models, with the best model (LTX-Video_†) reaching only 48.6% SR versus the 3D-DP baseline at 24.0%—a gain that may not translate to real robotic applications where physical accuracy is critical.

### 3. Performance Ceiling Dependence on Base Policies

The paper explicitly states: *"The agent's overall performance depends on both world-model fidelity and the strength of the proposal and revision policies."* This limitation is under-explored—all experiments use relatively simple VLM or heuristic policies as base proposals. The AdaWM review raises similar concerns about fairness in comparisons when the methods rely on different input modalities (perfect BEV perception versus raw sensors). World-In-World's improvements may be inflated if the base policies are weak rather than demonstrating genuine WM utility.

### 4. Computational Overhead for Real-Time Applications

The paper acknowledges that "incorporating world models into model-based planning introduces additional computational overhead because multiple future rollouts must be simulated at each decision step." Figure 7 shows SR increasing with more inferences (from ~53% with 3 inferences to ~61% with 11 inferences per episode), but this scaling requires substantial compute that may be impractical in real-time or resource-constrained embodied settings. The computational cost versus performance trade-off is not systematically analyzed.

### 5. Narrow Task Scope and Evaluation Scale

The benchmark covers only four tasks across two simulators, with relatively small evaluation sets (551 episodes for AR, 144 for ImageNav, 184 for A-EQA, 50 episodes per manipulation task). This limited scale raises questions about whether the three key "surprises" reported in the paper (controllability matters more than visual quality, action-conditioned post-training is more effective than upgrading generators, and inference-time compute improves performance) are broadly generalizable or specific to these particular task formulations and environments.

### 6. Post-Training Data Requirements May Limit Practical Utility

The paper demonstrates that scaling post-training data consistently improves performance (Figure 6), but the "data scaling law" requires 40K-80K instances to approach optimal performance. This substantial data requirement may limit practical utility for researchers or practitioners wanting to adapt world models to new, potentially small-scale embodied tasks or domains with limited available demonstration data.
</final_review>
