Now I have enough information to formulate my review. Let me compile the key weaknesses inspired by the human reviews.

<final_review>
## Weaknesses

**1. User Simulator Inherently Grounded in Ground-Truth SQL May Limit Benchmark Realism**

The paper explicitly states that "Each injected ambiguity is paired with a corresponding SQL snippet from the ground-truth query as a clarification source, which guides our user simulator in generating consistent and contextually appropriate clarifications" (Section 3.2). This means the user simulator's responses are fundamentally guided by the ground-truth SQL when answering clarification questions. In real-world scenarios, users do not have access to ground-truth SQL to inform their clarifications. This design introduces a risk that the clarification responses may subtly leak solution-relevant patterns through their phrasing or structure, even if the raw SQL is not revealed. A more realistic setup would decouple the simulator's responses entirely from the ground-truth solution.

> "Heavy reliance on GPT-generated dialogues introduces the risk of bias and synthetic errors inherent to the model, which could undermine the dataset's realism." — Human Review (ToolDial/J1J5eGJsKZ)

> "The dataset was constructed using an LLM (GPT4-o-mini) — perhaps some artifacts in generation can lead to undesirable effects." — Human Review (ToolDial/J1J5eGJsKZ)

**2. Single Experimental Runs Without Variance Estimation Undermine Result Reliability**

The paper states: "All models use temperature=0 and top_p=1, with default reasoning settings, conducting single runs due to cost" (Section 5, Appendix). With only a single run per model, there is no way to assess the statistical reliability of the reported performance differences. This is particularly concerning when comparing multiple models across two evaluation settings, as even temperature=0 models can exhibit subtle non-determinism under varying computational environments. The lack of confidence intervals or multiple runs makes it difficult to determine whether observed differences (e.g., GPT-5 at 8.67% vs. other models at lower rates) are statistically meaningful.

> "The experimental results lack clarity regarding whether they stem from the capabilities of the backbone model or the proposed framework." — Human Review (CHASE-SQL/CvGqMD5OtX)

**3. Narrow Task Scope of Exactly Two Sub-Tasks May Not Reflect Long-Horizon Real-World Interactions**

Each task in BIRD-INTERACT is constrained to exactly two sub-tasks (n=2): "a priority sub-task q₁ containing ambiguities" and "a follow-up sub-task q₂" (Section 2). While the overall interaction count per task averages 13+ turns (Table 1), the narrow two-sub-task structure limits the diversity of follow-up sequences. Real-world database assistance often involves more than two sequential sub-tasks with evolving user goals. Additionally, the follow-up sub-task categories are drawn from a single five-category taxonomy, which may not capture the full range of realistic user intent evolution. This constrained task design could overestimate performance on simpler interaction patterns while underestimating challenges in longer conversational trajectories.

> "The experiments in this work are still preliminary. Addition of more experimental details, clearly signifying the type of prompting baselines... can also be useful." — Human Review (ToolDial/J1J5eGJsKZ)

**4. Test Data Contamination Risk Due to Full Benchmark Release and Overlap with Existing Training Data**

The paper releases the full benchmark publicly ("we will publicly release all components under a permissive license"). Given that the tasks are built upon LIVESQLBENCH, which itself derives from BIRD and Spider datasets, many of the evaluated models (especially GPT-5, GPT-4o, and Claude models) likely contain significant overlap with the source datasets in their training data. Once the full test set is released, future model versions can be trained on these tasks, degrading the benchmark's validity over time. The paper does not discuss any private holdout set or dynamic update mechanism to mitigate this.

> "There is a risk of future data contamination. To mitigate this, it may be beneficial to release only the validation set while keeping the test set hidden, similar to practices used in NL2SQL benchmarks." — Human Review (BigCodeBench/YrycTjllL0)

> "No protection against future test data leakage: the full dataset is released meaning that once model training cutoffs move past the benchmark release date, the benchmark will not be usable anymore. Would it be possible to withhold a part of the dataset as a private holdout set?" — Human Review (CyBench/tc90LV0yRL)

**5. Ambiguity Annotation Conditioned on Ground-Truth SQL May Introduce Systematic Bias**

The paper states that implementation-level ambiguities "are annotated conditionally, i.e., only when the corresponding SQL operations are present in the ground-truth SQL" (Appendix H). This forward-looking design—where what counts as ambiguous is defined by whether it appears in the gold solution—creates a circular dependency between ambiguity annotation and the ground truth. This could systematically bias the benchmark toward evaluating model performance on ambiguities that happen to appear in the ground truth, rather than ambiguities that users naturally encounter in practice. A more robust approach would define ambiguities independently of the solution SQL.

> "Synthetic evaluation and test sets pose the risk of overestimating the performance of models. This may occur due to the lower diversity of natural language questions or the fact that, since they are generated from SQL, they may be too close to SQL or otherwise 'unnatural' compared to questions that users of such systems may ask." — Human Review (MMQA/GGlpykXDCa)

**6. Human Alignment Evaluation on Only 100 Tasks May Not Generalize to Full 900-Task Suite**

The human alignment study in Section 6 evaluates simulator-human correlation on "100 randomly sampled tasks across BI and DM domains." Given that the full benchmark contains 900 tasks (300 LITE + 600 FULL), a sample of 100 tasks represents only ~11% of the dataset. The reported 0.84 Pearson correlation (p=0.02) for GPT-4o may not generalize across the full distribution, especially for rare or challenging task types. This is particularly important because the user simulator is central to the entire evaluation framework—if the simulator does not accurately reflect human behavior across the full suite, the reported success rates may be unreliable.

> "It is unclear how many test samples were used... The presence of integer scores is unusual... Including precise sample counts and calculation methods could enhance transparency." — Human Review (ToolDial/J1J5eGJsKZ)
</final_review>
