<final_review>
## Weaknesses

1. **Missing comparison with existing dual-system methods**: The paper positions AgileThinker as novel compared to prior dual-process approaches (e.g., Zhang et al., 2025; Liu et al., 2024; Christakopoulou et al., 2024) but does not include these as baselines in experiments. As noted in a similar review: "It is hard to evaluate the contribution of this paper without a detailed comparison/discussion of these very related approaches" and "I think it is better to compare different methods with both objectives (performance and efficiency) in mind." [Reviewer 1, KgKN7F0PyQ.md, lines 13-16]

2. **Results presented without error bars despite observed variance**: The paper reports significance tests but does not include error bars or confidence intervals in its main result figures. This is problematic because "Figure 2 indicates a large variance. Why are there no error bars in the tables?" [Reviewer 3, o3V7OuPxu4.md, line 130]

3. **Limited game diversity for generalization claims**: The paper tests on only three games (Freeway, Snake, Overcooked) and three difficulty levels. This raises concerns similar to those expressed in another review: "the title implies a general answer...but only tests one game" and "[limited tasks] makes it harder to claim that the question is being comprehensively addressed." [Reviewer 4, 8Q0beBHq41.md, lines 145-150]

4. **Reactive budget T_R tuning procedure unclear**: While the paper provides an empirical method for finding T_R and mentions an adaptive algorithm, the procedure for determining optimal T_R across different environments is not fully automated and requires manual tuning. "The memory retrieval approach relies solely on cosine similarity without a discussion of its selection or adaptability as [conditions] scale." [Reviewer 3, KgKN7F0PyQ.md, lines 88-90]

5. **Missing ablation of dual-thread components**: The paper does not fully isolate the contribution of the parallel architecture versus other design choices. "It would be nice to see how much does the [specific component] alone contribute to the overall performance." [Reviewer 1, KgKN7F0PyQ.md, line 17]
</final_review>
