Based on my thorough analysis of the input paper and relevant human reviews from the dataset, here is the final review:

<final_review>
### Weaknesses

1. **Synthetic-to-Real-World Generalization Gap**: The paper's ClevrPolicy dataset is built on the synthetic CLEVR benchmark, which differs fundamentally from real-world scenarios. The performance gap between ClevrPolicy (up to 85.0% with ClevrPolicy-M) and GTAPolicy (63.21%-66.98%) highlights this limitation. Similar concerns were raised in reviews of synthetic data research (SynthCLIP, 7DY2Nk9snh): "purely synthetic image-caption datasets" may not translate well to real-world applications, and the paper does not adequately address whether the learned policy internalization would transfer to authentic multimodal policy-following scenarios beyond the controlled synthetic setting.

2. **LLM Judge Evaluation Without Bias Analysis**: The Policy Referral evaluation relies on Claude-4 as an LLM judge to score policy adherence, but the paper provides no analysis of potential judge biases. This echoes concerns from JudgeLM reviews (xsELpEPn4A) regarding LLM-based evaluation: "While the validation set was manually checked and corrected by the authors, it does still rely on GPT generated outputs... There is a reasonable chance that... humans checking responses would also consider them reasonable." The absence of bias controls (e.g., position bias, format bias, reference drop) for the Policy Referral evaluation makes it difficult to assess the reliability of the internalization assessment.

3. **Limited Real-World Data (451 Instances)**: GTAPolicy contains only 451 training instances for a real-world policy task, which is extremely small. The paper acknowledges this leads to overfitting issues with DAPO, yet the analysis of how well TriMPI generalizes from such limited data is shallow. This resonates with concerns from low-resource learning reviews (aMBSY2ebPw): working with extremely limited data "motivates the use of all available resources," but the paper doesn't thoroughly investigate failure modes or ablate what aspects of the training contribute to generalization in this data-starved regime.

4. **Insufficient Ablation on PolicyRollout Contribution**: While the paper shows that the RL stage provides the largest improvement (~50% over CoT SFT) and VM-CPT adds ~10%, the ablation on PolicyRollout shows only ~2-5% improvement. The explanation of "why this marginal gain matters" and what specifically it enables (more policy-grounded exploration) lacks depth. Similar concerns were raised in Q-SFT reviews (v4MTnPiYXY): "The experimental results were a bit too short. It would have been good to have more ablation studies to understand why the method works, and in which situations it is more optimal."

5. **Catastrophic Forgetting Evaluation on Limited Benchmarks**: The forgetting analysis only covers three downstream benchmarks (MMMU-Pro, MMLU-Pro, WildGuardTest), and the paper does not discuss what other general capabilities might be compromised. Reviews of continual learning work (gc8QAQfXv6) note that "forgetting will be model-dependent since performance on different datasets and tasks is model-dependent" and suggest evaluating across more diverse capability dimensions. The current evaluation may not capture the full spectrum of capability degradation from policy internalization.
</final_review>
