5. **Trade-off between recall and speed is underanalyzed**: The float32 HNSW baseline achieves 99.8% Recall@1 versus 97.2% for LAVQ—a 2.6 percentage point gap that is non-trivial for high-stakes retrieval applications. The paper presents this as acceptable but does not investigate whether the recall gap varies with efSearch, graph density (M), or dataset size. A reviewer on a related quantization paper observed: *"perplexity or cross-entropy loss are certainly reasonable proxies...but they are not perfectly correlated"* (wJv4AIt4sK.md)—similarly, QPS and Recall@1 together do not fully characterize retrieval quality.

</final_review>
